te in the law  such as gender race ethnicity or  disability  it usually only covers outcomes  so bias that might be there in  inputs or features is less of a concern  and again usually is focused on  group fairness so a minority group  suffering discrimination  is of concern rather than specific  individuals within that group  in the u.s there are three main types of  discrimination  that are outlawed in financial services  prohibited basis using protected  characteristics  like gender or race directly to make a  decision  so for example deciding only to lend to  men  disparate treatment not using a  protective characteristic  directly but still intentional  discrimination  against a group because of that  characteristic so  we've just seen an example which is  redlining which was  often a proxy for race and was  deliberately so  and disparate impact which is  unintentional  but still gives negative outcomes for  those people in a protected class versus  the majority default class  in some in some instances an example of  this  is education level if  individuals in a protected class have  had less opportunity to get access to  education  the 80 rule is often used as a threshold  to demonstrate whether discrimination  has taken place or not  it's not formally part of financial  services regulation  it comes from employment law but it has  been widely adopted in the industry  and it says that you should be hiring or  lending  to people in minority groups at at least  80 of the rate as the default group  so if we were hiring  or lending to people with disabilities  at  only 50 percent of the rate of the  default majority group then we would not  pass this test  in the uk the situation is a bit  different  the uk does have the equality act which  prohibits discrimination  on the basis of protected  characteristics again such as disability  or gender marital status race and others  but the uk regulator the financial  conduct authority  or fca has adopted a principles-based  regulatory regime in a principles-based  system  the principles that the fca wants  wants you to adhere to are defined but  is then left  to banks and others to decide how best  to implement them  so lenders are expected not to  discriminate  but there are a lot fewer specific rules  such as disparate impact or disparate  treatment as in  in the us in practice though  lenders often adhere to a very similar  approach  one of the principles that the fca does  think  important is the impact of any fairness  concern  i've pulled a few examples here from a  nice recent paper  by the fca looking at this  and talking about how much are  individuals harmed by this  if they're harmed minimally then that's  not so much of a concern  to the fca but if they're harmed  significantly that will be more of a  concern  how many people are harmed if it's just  a small minority then that's less of a  concern  whereas there is a significant group of  people that's more of a concern  and how are firms how are lenders  discriminating  if it's transparently and based on  changeable behavior  such as switching from one product to a  cheaper one  then that's less of a concern but if  it's base if it's hidden the  discrimination  and on the basis of intrinsic  characteristics then that is more of a  concern  again all of this is focusing on the  impact  of the of the uh the bias  that we're seeing here the scale and  severity of that  so how do we go about building a risk  model well  the typical approach of a large lender  would probably be something like this  they try to build a parsimonious model  building the  simplest version of the model that they  can  this is good practice anyway but is  particularly important here  as it will help a lot with validation  they will also tend to favor using small  simple pieces of  to construct their models as these are  easier to reason about  rather than doing it all in one big  black box  they will favor interpretable models  which  in practice