 very small amount  but the fairness metrics are now quite a  lot better  our equal opportunity true positive rate  ratio is now  79 so it nearly meets our threshold  though the predictive equality false  positive rate ratio  is still quite low at 70 percent  as a third experiment what if we replace  gender with the mean of that value when  we're making predictions  this lets the model use that protected  feature when training  so there's less chance that we'll try to  find proxies for janna  but then we remove it when the model is  being used live  the model performance is again very  similar to the previous one  so we haven't impacted our ability to  discriminate  but the equal opportunity is now much  better we get a 91  true positive rate ratio for men  compared to women  and predictive equality false positive  rate ratio has also improved a lot  to 88  this was a very quick analysis and i  skipped over a lot of important checks  that we'd want to do  on the model and data but it does show  that it's possible to get some pretty  dramatic improvements  in fairness without impacting the  overall performance  much at all  so which metrics should we use are equal  opportunity  and predictive equality a good choice  here  i think it depends i think  you have to relate it back to the wider  question  thinking about what the product is that  we're building here  what the demographic is that we're  targeting  about our use case for example  if we were offering mortgages there's an  argument that a false positive  is much more likely to be damaging than  a false negative we as a lender  would lose a lot of money potentially  and it's also likely that the borrower  would lose their home  it seems much safer to be conservative  and also important for fairness  that the false positive rate is no  higher  for the minority class  on the other hand if we were offering  loans  to people who may have difficulty  getting access to credit  otherwise then a false negative  may be more harmful as they may not have  many other opportunities to get that  credit  but generally it's a good idea to look  broadly  use multiple fairness metrics and think  about what impact  it may have on both you as a lender and  on your customers  if your model is biased for that metric  so as we've just discussed it's unlikely  that we'll be able to move bias  entirely from our models but there are  some other mitigations  that we can use no less harmful impact  is one we demonstrate that we can't  build  another model with less bias and similar  performance  using a different social features or a  different model building approach  age is an example here people who are  older  tend as a rule to have a longer credit  history  than younger people for obvious reasons  but a long credit history is also  something that is really quite  predictive  of being good credit risk so  its use is usually allowed and usually  quite  beneficial  comparing it to a reference model is  another way  we can mitigate so if your model has  some bias  but compared to your current model or  an industry standard model you can  demonstrate that there is  less bias then you are  at least improving the situation  compared to where things are now  the product use case is another  interesting  way of looking at this for example if  your model  is being used to give a second look  to reassess people who have been  rejected by  a main model then  if you're giving people a second chance  the worst case is  that they will be rejected again and the  best case is of course they will now be  accepted  so the outcome of the model is a net  positive  even if there is some bias in how it  performs  so the data set we've just looked at had  protective characteristics in it  but often this won't be the case and  if it's not the case then it's a lot  more difficult to check the bias  and i don't have any great answers to  this  unfortunately one of the main solutions  in this space is the cfpb the consumer  financial protection bureau  one of the u.s regulators who have  released a  bisg the bayesian improved  surname geocoding methodology  this is an industry standard and it is  widely used by lenders in the us  but it certainly has limitations  the surname-based approach has  difficulty  separating non-hispanic blacks from  non-hispanic whites because  their surnames are relatively uh similar  and  the geography-based  approach has difficulty identifying  hispanics and asians because those  groups  are not particularly spatially  segregated  so if we look ahead a little bit  i think we might be on the cusp of some  interesting and  possibly even quite profound changes in  finance  the typical approach of large  institutions at the moment  is not really using many  advanced machine learning techniques for  making lending decisions  major lenders are quite conservative and  careful  and regulators also need to be convinced  of this this approach um to make sure  that they're satisfied it's it's  uh safe and fair  um but this is starting to happen  um a number of fintechs are  starting to use this sort of approach  using more advanced techniques and big  lenders are  definitely paying attention to this  the regulators also have released a  number of papers  and consultations over the past few  years and  i'm quietly hopeful that over the next  year  or two or maybe three we will  start to get advice from them that this  sort of approach  is permitted and possibly even  encouraged  and there's a significant opportunity  if if that does happen there will be  more opportunities for giving access to  credit  for credit invisibles and others who  have  been shut out of a lot of the financial  system at the moment because  we can build more sophisticated models  using alternative data for example  and hopefully more accurate models as  well  we will be in a position hopefully to  explain the decisions of these models  for consumers and regulators through  surrogate models such as lime  or chap and  there'll also be opportunities to  hopefully emphasize  and focus a little bit more on  quantitative assessments  of features and data  and the fairness of those as well as the  quality of approaches at the moment  i think both of these are important and  it would be good to  encourage both  if we look ahead a little bit for  machine learning and  data science this is possibly not  what you want to hear as it's largely  about process  but a lot of the regulation in  financial services while onerous exists  to protect people from  bad outcomes and machine learning is  having an increasingly  large impact on our lives in many areas  now  and i think the things that we as data  scientists  and machine learning engineers can learn  from the checks and balances that  exist in finance adopting wholesale what  an international bank does would be  completely disproportionate for a  small startup or probably even for our  large tech unicorn  but having a good governance approach  will  go a long way to preventing bound  outcomes inadvertently happening  having a diverse team who care about  fairness is really important but  the responsibility for checking  adequately for buyers shouldn't just  fall on  their shoulders make it part of how  your company does things as well  like finance we as technologists and  as people building machine learning  models especially  are becoming a key part of many people's  lives  that's a real privilege but also a real  responsibility  so please check  your models are fair and  give the best outcomes for as many  people as possible  thank you i find the intersection  between  finance and tech those worlds are  a fascinating area so if you do too i'd  love to  discuss chat further hear ideas  and learn more because i still have a  lot to discover  so please get in touch via the contact  details  on here and the notebook is also  available on github at the link on the  slide  thanks again
 

